# Kubernetes Best Practices

Authoritative standards for raw Kubernetes manifests in this repo. For Helm
chart authoring (`Chart.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `templates/`, `_helpers.tpl`),
see `helm-best-practices.mdc` instead.

## General Configuration

- Use the latest stable API version (`kubectl api-resources` to check)
- Write manifests in YAML, not JSON
- Use only `true`/`false` for booleans — never `yes`/`no`/`on`/`off`
- Store all manifests in version control
- Group related objects (Deployment + Service + ConfigMap) in a single file separated by `---`
- Keep manifests minimal — omit redundant default values

## Naming and Labels

Apply the standard Kubernetes label set to every resource:

```yaml
metadata:
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: my-app
    app.kubernetes.io/instance: my-app-prod
    app.kubernetes.io/version: "1.2.0"
    app.kubernetes.io/component: backend
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: platform
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: helm
```

- Use lowercase kebab-case for label values
- Add `kubernetes.io/description` annotations to explain why a resource exists
- Use `kubernetes.io/change-cause` on Deployments to record rollout reasons (`kubectl rollout history` reads it)
- Never use `kubernetes.io/` or `k8s.io/` prefixes for **custom** labels — these are reserved for well-known keys (`app.kubernetes.io/*`, `pod-security.kubernetes.io/*`, etc.)

## Workloads

- Never create naked Pods — use Deployments, StatefulSets, or Jobs
- Set `replicas >= 2` for production Deployments and pair with a `PodDisruptionBudget` (`minAvailable: 1` minimum) to survive node drains
- Use `RollingUpdate` strategy with explicit `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable` (`maxUnavailable: 0` for zero-downtime services)
- Spread replicas across nodes/zones using `topologySpreadConstraints` or pod anti-affinity
- Pin images to immutable references — never `:latest` or other mutable tags. Prefer SHA256 digests in production
- Set `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent` for digest-pinned or version-pinned images; reserve `Always` for development

## Resource Management

Always set requests and limits for every container:

```yaml
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "256Mi"
```

- Set requests to average usage, limits to 2–3x requests
- For critical workloads, set requests = limits (Guaranteed QoS)
- Use `kubectl top pods` to measure actual consumption and tune values
- Apply `ResourceQuota` and `LimitRange` per namespace to prevent resource starvation

## Health Probes

Every container MUST define `readinessProbe` and `livenessProbe`. Add
`startupProbe` only for containers with slow / variable startup time
(migrations, JVM warmup, large model loads).

```yaml
startupProbe:           # only for slow-start containers
  httpGet:
    path: /healthz
    port: 8080
  failureThreshold: 30
  periodSeconds: 10
readinessProbe:         # required
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5
livenessProbe:          # required
  httpGet:
    path: /healthz
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 10
```

- `readinessProbe` gates traffic to pods that are truly ready
- `livenessProbe` restarts stuck containers
- `startupProbe`, when present, suspends liveness/readiness until it succeeds — preventing premature kills during slow init
- Use distinct paths/logic for liveness vs. readiness; a failing dependency should fail readiness (drain traffic), not liveness (kill the pod)
- Tune `initialDelaySeconds` and `failureThreshold` to actual measured startup time

## Security

### Pod Security Context

```yaml
securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  fsGroup: 1000
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault
```

### Container Security Context

```yaml
securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]
```

- Apply the Pod Security Standards "restricted" profile at the namespace level
- Create dedicated ServiceAccounts per workload — never rely on the `default` account
- Follow least-privilege RBAC: no wildcards in verbs or resources, prefer namespace-scoped Roles over ClusterRoles

## Networking

- Use `ClusterIP` services by default; `NodePort`/`LoadBalancer` only when needed
- Prefer the Gateway API (`HTTPRoute`, `Gateway`) over legacy `Ingress` resources
- Enforce TLS termination at the edge with Cert-Manager for automated certificate management
- Implement default-deny `NetworkPolicy` per namespace, then allow only required traffic:

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
```

## Namespaces

- Use separate namespaces for environments (`dev`, `staging`, `prod`) and teams
- Apply `ResourceQuota` per namespace
- Enforce Pod Security Standards via namespace labels:

```yaml
metadata:
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
```

## ConfigMaps and Secrets

- Store non-sensitive configuration in ConfigMaps, sensitive data in Secrets
- Never commit plaintext Secrets to version control — use Sealed Secrets, SOPS, or an external secrets manager (External Secrets Operator)
- Mount Secrets as volumes (with `defaultMode: 0400`) instead of environment variables when possible
- Use `immutable: true` on ConfigMaps/Secrets that do not change, for performance and safety
- Trigger pod restarts on config changes via a `checksum/config` annotation on the pod template

## Validation

Before applying manifests, validate them:

```bash
# Schema and best-practice validation
kubeconform -strict -summary manifest.yaml

# Server-side dry-run (catches admission webhook violations)
kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f manifest.yaml

# Lint with kube-linter or polaris for security/reliability checks
kube-linter lint manifest.yaml
```

